<section class="section">
  <div class="container owner-choice-grid">
    <div class="owner-choice-card">
      <p class="section-tag">Owner Math</p>
      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-bottom: 14px;">Why the fee conversation usually gets distorted</h2>
      <p>Owners love to compare one manager at 15%, another at 20%, and another at 25% as if that settles the decision. It does not. OTA commissions, discount-heavy pricing, and weak local execution can erase more owner income than the headline fee alone.</p>
      <p>The useful question is not "who is cheapest?" It is "who protects net owner income once pricing discipline, channel mix, guest standards, and local response all hit the same property?"</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Competitor fee bands often land around 18-35%, but the management percentage is only one part of the owner math</li>
        <li>Observed Airbnb host fees average roughly 13.4% versus direct payment cost closer to 2.9%</li>
        <li>Owner reporting should explain what changed in revenue, channel mix, and margin instead of just listing activity</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="owner-choice-card owner-proof-stat">
      <span class="owner-proof-label" style="color: var(--brand);">Commission Reality</span>
      <strong>13.4% → 2.9%</strong>
      <p>That is the difference between Seascape's observed Airbnb host-fee average and a direct payment cost. Owners who ignore channel mix are usually leaving real margin behind.</p>
      <p style="margin-top: 18px;">The teardown is designed to show whether your current manager is protecting rate, reducing fee drag, and tightening execution enough to justify what they charge.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

